WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:02.140
We are back to Konigsberg, that is now
Kaliningrad, Russia. 

00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:05.840
And you have now
learned how to

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.400
build a mega-city from millions of DNA reads and

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:12.970
how to navigate in this city to assemble
the genomes.

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:17.408
And it looks like a very simple and natural
city building approach 

00:00:17.408 --> 00:00:19.682
[UNKNOWN]

00:00:19.682 --> 00:00:24.290
AFTER, only AFTER we learned how to do
this.

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:24.654
But it took bioinformaticians 

00:00:24.654 --> 00:00:25.018
[UNKNOWN]

00:00:25.018 --> 00:00:26.370
two decades to figure out how to

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:28.956
apply Euler's idea to fragment assembly.  

00:00:28.956 --> 00:00:31.484
In fact, transforming real and often

00:00:31.484 --> 00:00:35.434
convoluted biological problems into
elegant algorithmic

00:00:35.434 --> 00:00:40.525
challenges is one of the most difficult
and rewarding tasks in bioinformaticsed.

00:00:40.525 --> 00:00:42.750
[INAUDIBLE]

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:46.440
When I walk today through Konigsberg, I
felt a sense of

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:51.660
adventure about Euler's theorem and how it
contributed to modern biology.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:56.600
Yet, biologists who invented DNA
sequencing had no idea about

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:01.084
Euler's result and how it could have
contributed to their studies.

00:01:01.084 --> 00:01:05.450
And likewise, Euler worked on a very

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:08.260
abstract mathematical problem and had no
idea, of course, 

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:13.750
that three centuries later, he 
would contribute to modern biology.

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:17.370
Our field trip is coming to an end. Now I

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:21.100
will let you assemble genomes on your own
using Euler's idea.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:25.480
As for me, it was a rainy morning in
Kaliningrad, and

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:29.250
I think I'll go for a walk with frequent
stops for cappuccino.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:33.610
Do you think it's possible to take a walk
in this city,

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:36.086
stopping in every COFFEEHOUSE exactly
once?

